# PART III — REAL GAMEPLAY SYSTEMS

## 7. Physics architecture

Physics must be an actual, testable part of the game.

Prefer a maintained physics engine for rigid-body simulation and collision handling. Do not imitate complex physics by manually moving meshes without a coherent collision model.

Separate simulation state from rendering state.

Use a fixed or appropriately controlled physics timestep with render interpolation where supported. Handle large frame-time spikes and tab suspension without allowing the simulation to become unstable.

Implement and test:

- Static world collision.
- Dynamic body collision.
- Player ground contact.
- Vehicle-to-world collision.
- Vehicle-to-vehicle collision.
- Character-to-world interaction.
- Projectile collision.
- Trigger volumes and interaction zones.
- Collision filtering and layer rules.
- Material-dependent friction and restitution where appropriate.
- Object reset and recovery.
- Cleanup of physics bodies and event listeners.

Maintain a documented physics configuration.

Do not use arbitrary collision responses that contradict the simulated state.

Where complex behavior cannot be modeled physically, implement a deterministic approximation and document it honestly.

## 8. Vehicle simulation

Implement at least two distinguishable vehicle configurations if practical, beginning with one fully functional vehicle.

Required controls:

- Acceleration.
- Braking.
- Reverse.
- Steering.
- Handbrake where appropriate.
- Enter and exit.
- Vehicle camera.
- Speed and gear feedback where applicable.
- Vehicle recovery.

Vehicle handling must account for:

- Mass and inertia.
- Speed-dependent steering.
- Acceleration and braking forces.
- Grip or traction.
- Lateral stability.
- Ground contact.
- Collision response.
- Orientation changes.
- Slope and terrain effects where supported.

A raycast-based vehicle controller is acceptable if it is stable and consistently implemented. A full rigid-body vehicle is also acceptable if it works reliably in the chosen engine.

Avoid sliding, uncontrolled spinning, unrealistic instantaneous stops, and vehicles that pass through world geometry.

### Damage and vehicle state

Implement a consistent vehicle damage model.

Collision severity may depend on relative velocity, effective mass, and collision impulse or another documented approximation.

Damage should affect defined vehicle states and, where implemented, handling or appearance.

Do not claim detailed deformation physics unless actual deformation is implemented.

### Vehicle acceptance tests

Verify:

- Forward and reverse movement.
- Braking and stopping.
- Steering response at different speeds.
- Vehicle collision with a wall.
- Collision with another vehicle.
- Enter/exit behavior.
- Recovery from a stuck state.
- Correct interaction with world collision.

