# PART X — BROWSER PERFORMANCE BENCHMARK

## 31. Performance targets

Target a stable, playable experience on an ordinary desktop PC.

Use a configurable quality system:

- Low.
- Medium.
- High.

Include settings for resolution scaling, shadows, post-processing, and NPC simulation density where practical.

Optimize through:

- Instanced rendering.
- Frustum culling.
- Efficient geometry and materials.
- Object pooling.
- Bounded AI updates.
- Spatial partitioning when appropriate.
- Controlled shadow-map resolution.
- Reduced unnecessary allocations.
- Efficient physics queries.
- Asset loading and disposal discipline.

Do not assume the user's PC has a dedicated GPU or unlimited memory.

## 32. Measurement protocol

When genuine browser measurement tools are available, record:

- Browser and version.
- Operating system if known.
- Screen resolution and render resolution.
- Graphics API and quality settings.
- NPC and active entity counts.
- Test scenario and duration.
- Average FPS.
- Frame-time percentiles, including p95 where measurable.
- Memory usage where reliably observable.
- Console errors and failed resource requests.
- Results before and after optimization.

Use reproducible scenarios:

A. Standing and camera movement.

B. Walking through a crowded street.

C. Driving through intersections.

D. Vehicle collision and recovery.

E. NPC-heavy simulation.

F. Combat and police pursuit.

G. Long-duration gameplay and cleanup.

A nominal goal is 60 FPS on a defined configuration, with a stable 30 FPS fallback target on weaker supported hardware.

These are targets, not guaranteed outcomes.

Never invent results. If a metric cannot be measured, report it as UNMEASURED.

## 33. Browser compatibility and recovery

Test the supported browser environment.

Handle:

- Unsupported rendering features.
- WebGL initialization failure.
- Asset-loading failure.
- Resize and orientation changes.
- Lost or restored rendering contexts where feasible.
- Browser tab suspension.
- Keyboard focus and pointer-lock behavior.
- Input cleanup when the game loses focus.
- Memory cleanup during restart or scene transitions.

Provide a useful error message instead of a blank screen whenever practical.

